@title Walk-in fridge alarm
@subtitle What volunteers do when the alarm sounds or the display reads above 41 °F
@author Sunset Valley Food Share, Operations Manager
@date 2026-11-01

kpi Alarm above | 41 °F | walk-in display | bad
kpi ChillRight (24 h) | 555-0134 | 4-hour response | neutral
kpi Restock below | 38 °F | for 4 hours | good

!!! Never taste or sniff food to decide whether it is safe.

# First two minutes
- Silence the alarm.
- Check the door is fully closed.
- Read the display and the backup thermometer inside.
- Write the time on the log sheet.

# Decide what to do
cards
### 41 to 45 °F, above 41 °F for less than 2 hours
- Move dairy, eggs, meat and prepared meals into the backup chest freezers right away.
- Keep produce in place.
- Call the refrigeration technician.
### Above 45 °F, or above 41 °F for 2 hours or more, or nobody knows how long
- Throw away dairy, eggs, meat and prepared meals, with a second volunteer present to confirm.
- Produce, canned and packaged goods can stay.

!! If nobody knows how long it has been above 41 °F, treat it as 2 hours or more.

# Steps in order
1. Call ChillRight, the refrigeration vendor, on 555-0134 (open 24 hours) and ask for a 4-hour response.
2. Message the Operations Manager.
3. Move or discard items as above.
4. Record each item thrown away on the waste log with its weight in pounds.
5. After the repair, run the refrigerator for 4 hours below 38 °F before restocking.

# Contacts
